For the default parameters, `N = 63` fits in `i`. Because the increment is blocking, the modulo test uses the **incremented** value of `i`. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/downloads/standards/v-ams/VAMS-LRM-2023.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (state != LOOP || i <= N); endproperty",
      "explanation": "In LOOP, i starts at zero and cannot advance beyond N. This rules out an unreachable i > N state when proving the transition to DONE."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (state != LOOP || sum == i / 9); endproperty",
      "explanation": "In LOOP, sum increments exactly when the updated i reaches a multiple of nine. Together with lemma_1, this invariant is preserved without counter wrap. At i = N = 9*n, it gives sum = n before the transition to DONE."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

These lemmas assume reset establishes the initial state; the quotient invariant relies on `N` fitting in `WIDTH` bits.
